  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1020 PM MDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nHOT REST OF THIS WEEK:\n\nThe high pressure ridge will continue to retrograde westward\nacross New Mexico and Arizona with its center south of the Four\nCorners. We will continue to recycle moisture underneath with\nPWAT anomalies holding steady at around 110 to 140 percent of \nnormal through Friday. Meanwhile, high temperatures will\ncontinue to remain around 5 to 10 degrees above normal. Forcing\nwill remain limited so expect storms to form over the high\nterrain each afternoon. Frequent lightning and gusty winds will\nbe possible from any storms that form, along with localized \nheavy rain. Should storms form over a sensitive area, or train \nover the same area, flash flooding will be possible, along with \ndebris flows associated with recent burn scars but this threat\ndoes appear localized through Friday. A little bit drier air\ndoes get pulled in as well by Friday with some gustier winds\nahead of a low pressure trough that moves into the Pacific\nNorthwest, which may result in localized critical fire weather \nconditions.\n\nCOOLER AND WETTER PATTERN THIS WEEKEND INTO NEXT WEEK:\n\nThe pattern does look to change once again by this weekend as\nthis low pressure trough moves into the Pacific Northwest, \nnudging the high to the east and shifting the flow to \nsouthwesterly. This will help draw up another surge of monsoonal\nmoisture as PWAT anomalies jump towards 160 to 180 percent of \nnormal. This low lifts across the Intermountain West and\nNorthern Rockies Saturday into Sunday, bringing some robust\nshortwaves through the moist, southwest flow which should result\nin more widespread showers and thunderstorms with heavy rain\npotential. This pattern looks to last through early next week\nas a large scale trough remains over the Pacific Northwest with\nseveral shortwaves moving through the base of this trough. So\nthis weekend into next week is trending wetter and cooler with\nhighs near to a few degrees below normal as we transition out of\nAugust and into the first week of September.",
